    "english": "I say: this too answers an objection that might be raised here. If unrestricted existence is predicated of the necessary reality and other existences by gradation and as an attribute, the necessary reality would have to exist twice—an evidently false consequence. The entailment is explained as follows. If existence is a graded, attached predicate, it must be a meaning common to the other existences and to that reality. Just as the other existences would exist twice—once insofar as they are existences in themselves, and again insofar as unrestricted existence attaches to them—the same would hold of the necessary reality. This is especially so if that reality is necessary through itself: it must efficiently cause everything else and therefore precede everything in existence, even the common meaning of existentness, since an efficient cause necessarily precedes its effect through existence.\n\nThe answer is that this follows only if the existentness common to existents is something externally different from that reality. It is not. Existentness is that very reality externally, although intellectually and mentally it is an additional attribute.\n\nIt should not be objected that, if common existentness is externally identical with that reality, the necessary reality itself would externally attach to quiddities—an evidently false consequence. That follows only if there is an external realization of the attachment just mentioned, which there is not. Common existentness has shares whose realities differ, and the relevant qualification obtains through each share. Consider walking in relation to its shares in humans, horses, and other things. The unrestricted meaning has no external existence within those quiddities distinct from its diverse shares, even though intellectually it has an independent occurrence, like those shares, and is conceived as attaching to them. Attachment belongs to the unrestricted meaning only with respect to its occurrence in intellect. Externally there are only the diverse shares; no such attachment occurs there.",
    "notes": [
      "The walking analogy concerns a general meaning and its instantiations. It is not evidence that the Necessary is an accident inhering in bodies; the text explicitly rejects that inference.",
      "The reply remains part of the exposition of the rival position. Its eventual criticism follows in SH-011 and subsequent sections.",
      "Mawjūdiyya is translated existentness here so that the argument's shift between wujūd and the predicable being-existent remains visible."
    ],
